{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>Behold the people, etc.</b> One needs to know why he altered the words of Balak, for Balak said <b>behold a people came out</b> (Numbers 22:5) while he said <b>behold the people who are coming out</b> (Numbers 22:11); Balak said <b>behold it covers</b> while he said <b>and it covers</b>; Balak said <b>and now</b> while he said <b>now</b>; Balak said <b>curse</b> while he said <b>enjoin</b>; Balak said <b>let us strike him</b> while he said <b>to do battle against him</b>. And it may be explained according to the words of our Sages of blessed memory (Numbers Rabbah 20:9), who said that Balaam was mistaken in his mind, thinking that there is a thing hidden from HaShem, heaven forbid, and he erred from the statement ״Who are the men…״ and so forth (Numbers 22:9). Even though we explained it there by another way, there are seventy faces to the Torah. And since his eye misled him in this, he thought to alter the words of Balak, because from them his hatred of Balaam would be heard, as I explained in connection with the statement ״behold a people came out.״ And Balaam was careful not to appear in the eyes of HaShem as one who hates Israel, for it was already publicized to all that the people of Israel are the beloved of the Master of the Place, and the episode of Egypt proves it. Therefore he altered and said <b>behold the people who are coming out of Egypt</b>, for as is known, the exodus from Egypt was publicized to the entire world, and in this language there is not heard what he explained in connection with the statement ״behold a people came out״ — that this would indicate Balaam's enmity. Also, he said <b>and it covers</b>, etc., and did not say ״behold it covers,״ for his own reason, to conceal the matter that would be heard from them — that he is a hater, as mentioned. Therefore he did not say ״behold,״ which would indicate making known the opposite of what was in their wish and knowledge regarding Israel, according to his counsel as I explained. And his saying <b>and it covers</b> — meaning, he informs him of the reason for the fear that comes upon him from them, for they are numerous." "And his saying <b>now come, enjoin</b> — meaning, he intended to come with the claim that justice is with Balak in this matter, for even though the Holy One, blessed be He, gave Ammon and Moab to Israel, still the time of the giving had not yet arrived, and justice is with him to do battle against him and to drive him out. And his saying <b>now</b> — meaning, at this time enjoin for me to do battle against him, to drive him out from upon me. According to this, there is a reason for the change of the word ״now,״ for if he had said ״and now״ it would be heard as a continuation of what preceded it, whereas he intended the claim of timing — that he is able to wage war against him now. Also the change to ״enjoin,״ for Balak's intention was not to curse the people in a general way, for then the evil would extend to the people both in the territory of Moab and in the territories of the other nations; rather, he would specify explicitly to Balaam that he does not curse him except in the measure of the war of Moab, so that Israel would be subdued, etc., before the Moabites. Therefore he was precise to say <b>enjoin for me</b> — meaning, that he should specify the curse as being for the measure of Moab. Also he changed in his saying ״to do battle against him״ in place of ״let us strike him,״ to say that he has no desire to do him harm but only to engage him in war to prevent him from his land. Also he changed and said <b>and I will drive him out</b>, whereas Balak said <b>from the land</b>. Perhaps Balak intended to drive him out from the known land that HaShem gave them, for once he subdues him they will fear to ascend to the land. And Balaam, to make Balak's words seem sweet and agreeable — for he seeks only his own benefit, with a claim that sounds reasonable and he is not an enemy of Israel — therefore he omitted the word ״from the land״ and said ״and I will drive him out״ — meaning, that he would drive him out from upon him."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {0 "The bracketed insertion of הָאָרֶץ ('the land') in the final lines of comment [1] appears to be a standard restoration; it is absent in the base text and restored from context."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:12:35.542251660Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}